---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BOVForm from '../../components/BOVForm.astro';
import ArticleFaq from '../../components/ArticleFaq.astro';
import { stripLinks } from '../../lib/inlineLinks';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

// Rates checked November 17, 2026. UPDATE EVERY JULY 1 (RSO) AND EVERY AUGUST 1 (AB 1482), and keep the numbers in the
// <script> below in step with these.
// - City of Los Angeles Housing Department (https://housing.lacity.gov/renter-protections-2 and /rso-rent-increase-calculator):
//   the RSO annual increase effective July 1, 2026 through June 30, 2027 is 3%, unless amended by the City Council. Council
//   amended the formula to 90% of average CPI, floor 1%, ceiling 4%, effective February 2, 2026; LAHD says the increase "remains
//   at 3% from July 1, 2025, to June 30, 2027." Since February 2, 2026 no utility add-on and no extra 10% for an added dependent.
//   RSO increases are allowed once every 12 months. State law requires 30 days' written notice for increases under 10%.
//   LAHD's registration bulletin says an owner cannot legally collect rent without a current RSO registration.
// - California Civil Code 1947.12 (AB 1482): the lesser of 5% plus the change in the regional CPI, or 10%, over any 12 months, measured
//   from the lowest rent charged in the prior 12 months; no more than two increments in 12 months. For increases taking effect on or
//   after August 1, the CPI change is April of that year against April of the year before. U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Los
//   Angeles-Long Beach-Anaheim CPI-U, 12-month change: April 2026 3.7% (cap 8.7%, increases from Aug 1, 2026), April 2025 3.0% (cap 8.0%).
// - Not covered by AB 1482 (Civil Code 1947.12(d)): housing under a stricter local rent control (so City of LA RSO buildings use the
//   RSO figure), deed-restricted affordable housing, housing with a certificate of occupancy within the last 15 years, and certain
//   single-family homes and owner-occupied duplexes.
// - City of Pasadena (Measure H, Charter Article XVIII; cityofpasadena.net/rent-stabilization): covers multi-unit rentals built before
//   February 1, 1995. The Annual General Adjustment (AGA) is 75% of the LA-area CPI for the 12 months ending in March, rounded to the nearest
//   quarter percent: 3.4% x 75% = 2.55%, rounded to 2.5%, for October 1, 2026 through September 30, 2027 (City Manager release, August 31,
//   2026). Earlier: 2.25% (Oct 2025 to Sep 2026), 3% (Oct 2024 to Sep 2025), 2.75% (Oct 2023 to Sep 2024). One increase per 12 months, written
//   notice (30 days), no stacking of banked increases.
// - City of Santa Monica Rent Control Board (santamonica.gov/maximum-lawful-rent): 2026 General Adjustment (GA) 2.6% effective September 1, 2026
//   with a $70 ceiling for MARs of $2,674 and above; the GA is based on 75% of the LA-area CPI change for the 12 months ending in March
//   (3.4% x 75% = 2.55%, announced as 2.6%). Applies to the Maximum Allowable Rent (MAR), only to tenancies that began before September 1 of the
//   prior year, with 30 days' written notice. The history table below is the Agency's "General Adjustment and Registration Fee History"
//   (PDF dated July 2025) plus the 2026 figures from the Maximum Lawful Rent page. Registration fee 2026/27: $240 a unit, $10 a month pass-through.
// - City of West Hollywood (weho.org/city-government/rent-stabilization): the Annual General Adjustment is 75% of the area CPI, announced by the
//   Rent Stabilization Commission before July 1 and applied September 1 through August 31; 75% of area inflation, nearest quarter point, 3% ceiling;
//   2.75% for Sept 1, 2026 to Aug 31, 2027 was announced at the June 25, 2026 Commission meeting (as on the WestHollywoodGuide); one increase per 12 months; it cannot be banked;
//   covers multi-family rentals built before July 1, 1979. As of November 18, 2026 the city's pages still showed 2.25% for September 1, 2025
//   through August 31, 2026 and did not show the 2026-27 percentage (a notice form "for increases effective September 1, 2026" exists). Until
//   the figure is confirmed the calculator asks the visitor to enter it. When confirmed, set it as the WeHo limit in the script and header.
// - The refinance figures are an illustration only: loan size is the extra income divided by (DSCR x the loan constant for the
//   chosen rate over a 30-year amortization). Lenders also cap loans by loan-to-value, so the lower of the two limits applies.
const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'How much can I raise the rent on a City of Los Angeles RSO unit in 2026?',
		a: 'The Los Angeles Housing Department (LAHD) publishes 3% for increases effective July 1, 2026 through June 30, 2027. Increases are allowed once every 12 months, and state law requires 30 days of written notice for an increase under 10%. Since February 2, 2026 an owner can no longer add a separate percentage for utilities. The City Council changed the formula to 90% of average CPI, with a floor of 1% and a ceiling of 4%, but LAHD says the rate stays at 3% through June 30, 2027.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Does the state 8.7% limit apply to my rent-controlled building?',
		a: 'No. California’s AB 1482 does not apply to housing that is covered by a local rent control law that limits annual increases to less than the state limit, so a City of Los Angeles RSO building follows the RSO percentage. AB 1482 matters for buildings that are not rent-stabilized, such as most apartment buildings built after October 1, 1978 in the City of Los Angeles, and for buildings in cities with no rent control of their own.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How is the AB 1482 limit figured?',
		a: 'It is 5% plus the percentage change in the regional Consumer Price Index, or 10%, whichever is lower, measured against the lowest rent charged for the unit in the prior 12 months. For increases that take effect on or after August 1, 2026, the Los Angeles-Long Beach-Anaheim CPI change from April 2025 to April 2026 was 3.7%, so the limit is 8.7%. Before August 1, 2026, it was 8.0%. If the same tenant stays, no more than two increases are allowed in a 12-month period.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How much can I raise the rent in Pasadena, and how is the limit calculated?',
		a: 'Pasadena’s Measure H limits increases in multi-unit rentals built before February 1, 1995. The Annual General Adjustment is 75% of the change in the Los Angeles-area CPI for the 12 months ending in March, rounded to the nearest one-quarter percent. For 2026 that was 3.4% times 75%, or 2.55%, rounded to 2.5%, for increases effective October 1, 2026 through September 30, 2027. It is allowed once every 12 months with written notice, and past increases that were skipped cannot be stacked on top of it. On a $2,000 rent, 2.5% is $50 a month.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How does Santa Monica rent control work, and what is the MAR?',
		a: 'Santa Monica limits rent by each unit’s Maximum Allowable Rent (MAR), the legal ceiling, which is not always the rent being charged. Each year the Rent Control Board announces a general adjustment based on 75% of the change in the Los Angeles-area CPI for the 12 months ending in March, with a dollar ceiling set by formula. For September 1, 2026 it is 2.6% with a $70 ceiling for MARs of $2,674 or more, so on a $3,000 MAR the increase is $70, not $78. It applies only to tenancies that began before September 1, 2025, and needs 30 days of written notice. The table above shows the adjustment for each year since 2015.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How much can I raise the rent in West Hollywood?',
		a: 'West Hollywood’s rent stabilization covers multi-family rentals built before July 1, 1979. The Annual General Adjustment is 75% of the change in LA-area inflation, rounded to the nearest quarter point, and can never exceed 3%. The Rent Stabilization Commission announces it each year before July 1 and it applies September 1 through August 31. For September 1, 2026 through August 31, 2027 it is 2.75%, up from 2.25% the year before. An increase is allowed once every 12 months with proper written notice, and an adjustment you skip cannot be banked and added later. The city’s rent page still showed the prior year’s figure when we checked on November 18, 2026, so confirm against your notice form or call the Rent Stabilization Division at (323) 848-6450. On a $2,000 rent, 2.75% is $55 a month.',
	},
	{
		q: 'I haven’t raised the rent in years. Can I catch up?',
		a: 'Not all at once. Under AB 1482 the limit applies to any 12-month window, so one increase cannot exceed 5% plus CPI or 10%. For RSO units LAHD publishes one percentage for each year and allows one increase every 12 months, and we have not found a rule that lets an owner add up the years that were skipped. Confirm your situation with LAHD or a landlord-tenant attorney before you send a notice. The practical point is that income you did not collect in earlier years is generally gone, which is why a long gap is expensive.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Do rent increases really change what my building is worth?',
		a: 'They can. Buyers and appraisers value an apartment building on its net operating income, so extra rent that is actually collected, with no matching rise in expenses, adds value at the market cap rate. The calculator shows that math. It is a simplification: it ignores vacancy and turnover costs, and an increase that pushes a good tenant out can cost you more than it earns. See what a [free Broker Opinion of Value](/broker-opinion-of-value/) says about your building in today’s market.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How do rent increases help if I plan to keep the building and refinance?',
		a: 'Lenders size a loan from the building’s income, usually by the lesser of a loan-to-value limit and a debt service coverage requirement. Higher collected rents raise the income that supports the loan, so a refinance can produce more proceeds. Lenders generally look at the current rent roll, leases and recent collections, so increases that were never sent, or sent incorrectly, may not count. The calculator’s refinance estimate is an illustration; ask your lender for actual terms.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Is this legal advice?',
		a: 'No. It is an informational estimate. Rent rules change, have exceptions, and differ by city, and an increase notice that is wrong can be invalid. Check the figures with LAHD or a landlord-tenant attorney before you act. If you want to talk through what the numbers mean for your building’s value, [call us or request a free valuation](#bov-form).',
	},
];

const faqSchema = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'FAQPage',
	mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
		'@type': 'Question',
		name: f.q,
		acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: stripLinks(f.a) },
	})),
};

// Santa Monica Rent Control Agency, "General Adjustment and Registration Fee History" (July 2025), plus the 2026 figures from the
// Maximum Lawful Rent page. Rows copied as published; 2022 shows the special mid-year changes the Agency lists under that year.
const smHistory = [
	{ year: '2026', ga: '2.6%', ceiling: '$70 (MARs of $2,674 or above)', fee: '$240 ($10.00)' },
	{ year: '2025', ga: '2.3%', ceiling: '$60 (MARs of $2,587 or above)', fee: '$240 ($10.00)' },
	{ year: '2024', ga: '3.0%', ceiling: '$76 (MARs of $2,517 or above)', fee: '$228 ($9.50)' },
	{ year: '2023', ga: '2.8%', ceiling: '$67 (MARs of $2,375 or above)', fee: '$228 ($9.50)' },
	{
		year: '2022',
		ga: '3.0%, then 6% for 9/1/22 to 1/31/23, then 0.8% for 2/1/23 to 8/31/23',
		ceiling: '$70 (MARs of $2,313 or above); $140 max for MARs of $2,325 or above (6%); $19 max for MARs of $2,313 or above (0.8%)',
		fee: '$228 ($9.50)',
	},
	{ year: '2021', ga: '1.7%', ceiling: '$39 (MARs of $2,265 or above)', fee: '$198 ($8.25)' },
	{ year: '2020', ga: '1.4%', ceiling: '$32 (MARs of $2,250 or above)', fee: '$198 ($8.25)' },
	{ year: '2019', ga: '2.0%', ceiling: '$44 (MARs of $2,175 or above)', fee: '$198 ($8.25)' },
	{ year: '2018', ga: '2.9%', ceiling: '$60 (MARs of $2,052 or above)', fee: '$198 ($8.25)' },
	{ year: '2017', ga: '2.0%', ceiling: '$40 (MARs of $1,975 or above)', fee: '$198 ($8.25)' },
	{ year: '2016', ga: '1.3%', ceiling: '$25 (MARs of $1,885 or above)', fee: '$174 ($7.29)' },
	{ year: '2015', ga: '0.4%', ceiling: '$7 (MARs of $1,875 or above)', fee: '$174 ($7.29)' },
];

const rentGapExamples = [
	{
		href: '/case-studies/453-s-gramercy-pl-koreatown-apartments/',
		title: '453 S Gramercy Pl, Koreatown',
		fact: '14 units, rent-controlled. Most tenants were long-term occupants paying rents about 80% below market.',
		result:
			'That gap made a sale at full value complicated. We found an off-market buyer experienced with below-market rental buildings, and the building sold for $2,200,000 in January 2017.',
	},
	{
		href: '/case-studies/925-n-san-vicente-blvd-west-hollywood-apartments/',
		title: '925 N San Vicente Blvd, West Hollywood',
		fact: '9 units, rent-controlled, built in 1963. Rents ran roughly 60% below market, alongside a required seismic retrofit and deferred maintenance.',
		result: 'We reached out to buyers who had shown interest in comparable buildings and went under contract in under a week.',
	},
	{
		href: '/case-studies/941-normandie-ave-east-hollywood-apartments/',
		title: '941 N Normandie Ave, East Hollywood',
		fact: '7 units, not rent-controlled. Rents ran roughly 45% below market, with extensive deferred maintenance.',
		result: 'We went directly to buyers of comparable non-rent-controlled buildings nearby. It closed at 97% of list price.',
	},
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Rent Increase Calculator: Value and Refinance Impact"
	description="Free calculator: 2026 rent increase limits for LA, Pasadena, Santa Monica and West Hollywood, and what annual increases add to income, value and refinancing."
>
	<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} slot="head" />

	<section class="ri-hero">
		<div class="container ri-hero__inner">
			<span class="ri-hero__eyebrow">Free Tool</span>
			<h1>Rent Increase Upside Calculator</h1>
			<p class="ri-hero__lede">
				See what the legal rent increase could add to your building&rsquo;s income, its value, and the loan you could get if you refinance, for the City of Los Angeles, Pasadena, Santa Monica and West Hollywood. It also shows what skipping increases costs over five years.
			</p>
			<ul class="ri-hero__chips" role="list">
				<li>2026 limits, checked against official sources</li>
				<li>Free, nothing to sign up for</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="ri-main">
		<div class="container ri-grid">
			<div class="ri-card">
				<div class="ri-card__head">
					<h2>Your Building</h2>
					<p>Change any number and the results update.</p>
				</div>
				<form id="ri-form" novalidate onsubmit="return false">
					<div class="ri-field">
						<label for="ri-type">Where is the building?</label>
						<select id="ri-type">
							<option value="rso" selected>City of Los Angeles, rent-stabilized (RSO)</option>
							<option value="state">City of Los Angeles, not rent-stabilized (California AB 1482 limit)</option>
							<option value="pasadena">Pasadena (Measure H)</option>
							<option value="sm">Santa Monica (Rent Control, MAR)</option>
							<option value="weho">West Hollywood (Rent Stabilization)</option>
						</select>
						<span class="ri-hint" id="ri-type-hint"></span>
					</div>
					<div class="ri-row">
						<div class="ri-field">
							<label for="ri-units">Number of units</label>
							<input id="ri-units" type="number" inputmode="numeric" min="2" max="500" step="1" value="12" />
						</div>
						<div class="ri-field">
							<label for="ri-rent" id="ri-rent-label">Average monthly rent per unit</label>
							<div class="ri-money"><span aria-hidden="true">$</span><input id="ri-rent" type="text" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" value="1,650" /></div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="ri-row">
						<div class="ri-field">
							<label for="ri-pct">Increase you plan to give (%)</label>
							<input id="ri-pct" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.1" value="3" />
							<span class="ri-hint" id="ri-pct-hint">The limit is 3%.</span>
						</div>
						<div class="ri-field">
							<label for="ri-cap">Market cap rate (%)</label>
							<input id="ri-cap" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="2" max="12" step="0.25" value="5.5" />
							<span class="ri-hint">Used to turn extra income into value. See our <a href="/market-report/">market report</a> for current cap rates.</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<details class="ri-more">
						<summary>Refinance assumptions</summary>
						<div class="ri-row">
							<div class="ri-field">
								<label for="ri-rate">Loan interest rate (%)</label>
								<input id="ri-rate" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="2" max="15" step="0.125" value="6.5" />
							</div>
							<div class="ri-field">
								<label for="ri-dscr">Lender coverage ratio (DSCR)</label>
								<input id="ri-dscr" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="1" max="2" step="0.05" value="1.25" />
							</div>
						</div>
						<span class="ri-hint">Illustrative only: a 30-year amortizing loan sized by coverage ratio. Ask your lender for real terms.</span>
					</details>
				</form>
				<p class="ri-disclaimer">
					Informational estimate only, not legal, tax or lending advice. It assumes the extra rent is collected and expenses stay the same.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="ri-card ri-card--result" aria-live="polite">
				<span class="eyebrow">If You Give the Increase</span>
				<p class="ri-big" id="ri-extra-year">$0</p>
				<p class="ri-sub" id="ri-extra-sub">more income every year</p>
				<div class="ri-flag" id="ri-flag" hidden></div>
				<dl class="ri-stats">
					<div><dt>Extra rent, per unit per month</dt><dd id="ri-per-unit">$0</dd></div>
					<div><dt>Added building value at your cap rate</dt><dd id="ri-value">$0</dd></div>
					<div><dt>Added loan proceeds if you refinance</dt><dd id="ri-loan">$0</dd></div>
					<div><dt>Notice required</dt><dd id="ri-notice">30 days</dd></div>
				</dl>
				<a href="#bov-form" class="ri-btn">See What Your Building Is Worth &rarr;</a>
				<a class="ri-call" href={`tel:+1${site.phone.replace(/\D/g, '')}`}>Or call {site.phone}</a>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="container">
			<div class="ri-card ri-skip">
				<h2>The Cost of Skipping Increases</h2>
				<p class="ri-skip__lede" id="ri-skip-lede"></p>
				<div class="ri-table-wrap">
					<table class="ri-table">
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th scope="col">Year</th>
								<th scope="col">Rent per unit if raised every year</th>
								<th scope="col">Rent per unit if never raised</th>
								<th scope="col">Rent you did not collect, running total</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody id="ri-skip-body"></tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
				<p class="ri-skip__note" id="ri-skip-note"></p>
			</div>
			<aside class="ri-bookmark" aria-label="Bookmark this page">
				<h2>Bookmark this page and check back</h2>
				<p>
					We keep this calculator current. The rent limits are updated when they change each year: July for City of Los Angeles rent-stabilized buildings,
					August for California&rsquo;s AB 1482 limit, September for Santa Monica and West Hollywood, and October for Pasadena. For the market side of the numbers, our <a href="/property-value/">free value calculator</a> and
					<a href="/market-report/">monthly market report</a> are refreshed regularly with new closed LA sales, so a fresh look every few months can change
					what your building is worth.
				</p>
			</aside>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-alt">
		<div class="container ri-text">
			<span class="eyebrow">The Rules</span>
			<h2>What the Law Allows Right Now</h2>
			<p>Checked against official sources on November 17, 2026. These limits change every year, so confirm them before you send a notice.</p>
			<div class="ri-rules">
				<div class="ri-rule">
					<h3>City of Los Angeles RSO</h3>
					<p class="ri-rule__num">3%</p>
					<p class="ri-rule__when">For increases effective July 1, 2026 through June 30, 2027</p>
					<ul>
						<li>One increase every 12 months.</li>
						<li>30 days of written notice for an increase under 10%.</li>
						<li>No separate utility add-on since February 2, 2026.</li>
						<li>The City Council changed the formula to 90% of average CPI, between 1% and 4%. LAHD says the rate stays at 3% through June 30, 2027.</li>
						<li>The unit has to be registered with LAHD before rent can be collected.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="ri-rule">
					<h3>California AB 1482</h3>
					<p class="ri-rule__num">8.7%</p>
					<p class="ri-rule__when">For increases effective August 1, 2026 through July 31, 2027</p>
					<ul>
						<li>The lesser of 5% plus regional CPI, or 10%. LA-area CPI rose 3.7% from April 2025 to April 2026.</li>
						<li>Before August 1, 2026 the limit was 8.0% (5% plus 3.0%).</li>
						<li>No more than two increases in 12 months, measured from the lowest rent charged in the prior 12 months.</li>
						<li>Does not apply to RSO buildings, deed-restricted affordable housing, or buildings with a certificate of occupancy in the last 15 years. Some single-family homes and owner-occupied duplexes are exempt.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="ri-rules ri-rules--cities">
				<div class="ri-rule">
					<h3>Pasadena, Measure H</h3>
					<p class="ri-rule__num">2.5%</p>
					<p class="ri-rule__when">For increases effective October 1, 2026 through September 30, 2027</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Covers multi-unit rentals built before February 1, 1995.</li>
						<li>One increase every 12 months, with 30 days of written notice.</li>
						<li>Skipped or banked increases cannot be added on top of the yearly limit.</li>
						<li>A rent change has to be registered with the city within 30 days.</li>
						<li>Earlier limits: 2.25% (October 2025 to September 2026), 3% (2024 to 2025) and 2.75% (2023 to 2024).</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="ri-rule">
					<h3>Santa Monica, Rent Control</h3>
					<p class="ri-rule__num">2.6%, $70 max</p>
					<p class="ri-rule__when">For increases effective September 1, 2026</p>
					<ul>
						<li>The percentage applies to the unit&rsquo;s Maximum Allowable Rent (MAR), the legal ceiling, which is not always the rent being charged.</li>
						<li>The increase is capped at $70 a month for MARs of $2,674 or more.</li>
						<li>Only for tenancies that began before September 1, 2025, with 30 days of written notice.</li>
						<li>Fees must be paid and the tenancy registered. Half of the $240 annual registration fee ($10 a month) may be passed to the tenant, separate from the MAR.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="ri-rule">
					<h3>West Hollywood, Rent Stabilization</h3>
					<p class="ri-rule__num">2.75%</p>
					<p class="ri-rule__when">For increases effective September 1, 2026 through August 31, 2027</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Covers multi-family rentals built before July 1, 1979.</li>
						<li>One increase every 12 months. An increase you skip cannot be banked and added later.</li>
						<li>The percentage is 75% of the change in LA-area inflation, rounded to the nearest quarter point, and never more than 3%. The Rent Stabilization Commission announced 2.75% at its June 25, 2026 meeting. The city&rsquo;s rent page still showed the prior year&rsquo;s 2.25% when we checked on November 18, 2026, so check your notice form or call the city at (323) 848-6450.</li>
						<li>The owner has to be current on the $144 per unit registration fee. The city is also considering updates to its ordinance.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3 class="ri-sub-h">How each limit is calculated</h3>
			<div class="ri-table-wrap">
				<table class="ri-table ri-table--rules">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">Place</th>
							<th scope="col">How the limit is set</th>
							<th scope="col">The 2026 numbers</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">City of Los Angeles (RSO)</th>
							<td>Published each year by the Housing Department. The new formula is 90% of average CPI, with a floor of 1% and a ceiling of 4%.</td>
							<td>The rate stays at 3% through June 30, 2027.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">California AB 1482</th>
							<td>5% plus the regional CPI change from April to April, but never more than 10%.</td>
							<td>5% + 3.7% = 8.7%.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Pasadena</th>
							<td>75% of the LA-area CPI change for the 12 months ending in March, rounded to the nearest one-quarter percent.</td>
							<td>3.4% &times; 75% = 2.55%, rounded to 2.5%. On a $2,000 rent that is $50 a month.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Santa Monica</th>
							<td>75% of the LA-area CPI change for the 12 months ending in March, applied to the MAR, with a dollar ceiling set by formula each year.</td>
							<td>3.4% &times; 75% = 2.55%, announced as 2.6%. On a $3,000 MAR, 2.6% is $78, so the $70 ceiling applies.</td>
