	<section class="section">
		<div class="container ri-text">
			<span class="eyebrow">The Trade-Offs</span>
			<h2>Giving Annual Increases, or Not: The Pros and Cons</h2>
			<p>
				Neither choice is right for every owner or every tenant. A good tenant you want to keep, a hardship, or a soft rental market can all be
				reasons to hold off. The point is to make it a decision with the numbers in front of you, not a habit.
			</p>
			<div class="ri-compare">
				<div class="ri-compare__col">
					<h3>If you give annual increases</h3>
					<h4>Pros</h4>
					<ul>
						<li>Income keeps pace with taxes, insurance, repairs and payroll, which rise every year.</li>
						<li>Every collected dollar of rent adds to net operating income, and buyers, appraisers and lenders price that income.</li>
						<li>Small, regular increases avoid a large gap that is harder to explain to a tenant or a buyer later.</li>
						<li>A documented rent history is easy for a lender or buyer to verify.</li>
					</ul>
					<h4>Cons</h4>
					<ul>
						<li>Some tenants may leave. A vacancy costs lost rent, turnover repairs and leasing time.</li>
						<li>It can strain a good tenant relationship, especially in a soft market.</li>
						<li>It adds paperwork. A wrong notice, a missed registration or an increase over the limit can make the increase invalid.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="ri-compare__col">
					<h3>If you hold the rent flat</h3>
					<h4>Pros</h4>
					<ul>
						<li>Goodwill, longer tenancies and fewer vacancies.</li>
						<li>Less administration, and no notices to get wrong.</li>
						<li>A sensible choice for a tenant who pays on time and takes care of the unit, if you make it deliberately.</li>
					</ul>
					<h4>Cons</h4>
					<ul>
						<li>The income you did not collect is generally gone. Limits apply to each 12-month window, so skipped years are not simply added back.</li>
						<li>The gap to market rent grows, and the building is worth less on paper because buyers and lenders value the rents in place.</li>
						<li>Expenses keep rising, so net income shrinks even though nothing about the building changed.</li>
						<li>A large gap complicates a sale at full value. It narrows the buyers who will pay for it, as the examples below show.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-alt">
		<div class="container ri-text">
			<span class="eyebrow">Keeping the Building</span>
			<h2>Why Increases Matter When You Plan to Refinance</h2>
			<p>
				If you are not selling, the rents you collect still decide what the building can borrow. Lenders typically size a loan two ways, by a
				loan-to-value limit and by a debt service coverage ratio, and the lower number wins. Coverage is measured against net operating income, and
				income is read from your current rent roll, your leases and what you actually collected.
			</p>
			<ul class="ri-points">
				<li><strong>More collected rent supports a bigger loan.</strong> The calculator above shows the added proceeds for your numbers. The same extra income also raises the appraised value, which helps the loan-to-value test.</li>
				<li><strong>Increases only count once they are in effect.</strong> Rent that could have been charged, but was not, generally does not count toward the loan.</li>
				<li><strong>Clean paperwork helps.</strong> A lender or buyer can ask for leases, notices and the rent roll. Increases that were sent correctly, on time, are easier to stand behind.</li>
				<li><strong>Timing matters.</strong> An increase you give a year or two before you refinance has time to show up in your collections.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="ri-note">
				This is a general explanation. Loan terms depend on the lender, the property and the market, so ask your lender or mortgage broker for real figures.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container ri-text">
			<span class="eyebrow">From Our Closed Sales</span>
			<h2>Real Buildings With Large Rent Gaps</h2>
			<p>
				These are buildings we sold where rents had fallen far below market. The reasons differ, from rent control to long tenancies, and we are not
				saying the owner made a mistake in any of them. What they show is how a big gap shapes a sale: who will buy, how the building is marketed, and
				what the owner has to work through.
			</p>
